ry (Lindzey, 18 Jan 2026).\n\nReferences (1)\n\n1.\n\nAn Eventown Result for Permutations  (2026)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCanadian Discrete and Algorithmic Mathematics (https://canadam.org/Program/CanaDAM2025Program.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Even-cycle-intersecting permutations ... We say that a family F ⊆Sn of permutations is even-cycle-intersecting if |σπ−1| ≡0 mod 2 for all σ, π ∈F.\nEven-cycle-intersecting permutations\nNathan Lindzey\nCM1\nDepartment of Mathematical Sciences, University of Memphis\nWe say that a family F ⊆Sn of permutations is even-cycle-intersecting if |σπ−1| ≡0 mod 2 for all σ, π ∈F.\nWe show that if F ⊆Sn is even-cycle-intersecting, then |F| ≤2n−1. The bound is met with equality when n is\na power of 2 and F is a Sylow 2-subgroup of Sn.\nMaximum leaf spanning arborescence in DAGs\nCarla Negri Lintzmayer\nCM24\nCenter for Mathematics, Computing and Cognition, Federal University of ABC, Brazil\nAssuming that P ̸= NP, a wide variety of optimization problems cannot be solved exactly in polynomial time.\nApproximation algorithms are a way to deal with these NP-hard problems. They are eﬃcient algorithms that\nprovide theoretical guarantees for the ratio between the cost of the solution found by the algorithm and the\ncost of an optimal solution to the problem. In this talk, I will show some results on approximation algorithms\nfor the problem of Maximum Leaf Spanning Arborescence in DAGs. This problem consists of, given a rooted\nacyclic digraph, ﬁnding a spanning arborescence with the largest number of leaves. The results I will show are\npart of a joint work with Prof. Cristina Gomes Fernandes, from the University of São Paulo.\nFast state transfer via loop weights\nGabor Lippner\nCM20\nDepartment of Mathematics, Northeastern University\nInspired by a related construction of David Feder et al, I will explain how to achieve quantum state transfer\nbetween ends of a spin chain in polynomial time of its length by putting loop weights on the second and second\nto last nodes. It turns out this protocol can be fully solved analytically and allows us achieve the same time\nscaling as the construction of Feder et al. Joint work with Yujia Shi.\n170--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Eventown Result for Permutations (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.12613)\nciteturn3academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Authors: Nathan Lindzey\nDate: Sun Jan 18 23:08:18 2026 ... We show that if $\\mathcal{F} \\subseteq S_n$ is an even-cycle-intersecting family of permutations, then $|\\mathcal{F}| \\leq 2^{n-1}$, and that equality holds when $n$ is a power of 2 and $\\mathcal{F}$ is a double-translate of a Sylow 2-subgroup of $S_n$.\nTitle: An Eventown Result for Permutations\nAuthors: Nathan Lindzey\nDate: Sun Jan 18 23:08:18 2026\n\nA family of permutations $\\mathcal{F} \\subseteq S_n$ is even-cycle-intersecting if $σπ^{-1}$ has an even cycle for all $σ,π\\in \\mathcal{F}$. We show that if $\\mathcal{F} \\subseteq S_n$ is an even-cycle-intersecting family of permutations, then $|\\mathcal{F}| \\leq 2^{n-1}$, and that equality holds when $n$ is a power of 2 and $\\mathcal{F}$ is a double-translate of a Sylow 2-subgroup of $S_n$. This result can be seen as an analogue of the classical eventown problem for subsets and it confirms a conjecture of János Körner on maximum reversing families of the symmetric group. Along the way, we show that the canonically intersecting families of $S_n$ are also the extremal odd-cycle-intersecting families of $S_n$ for all even $n$. While the latter result has less combinatorial significance, its proof uses an interesting new character-theoretic identity that might be of independent interest in algebraic combinatorics.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n27th Conference of the International Linear Alge